  "slug": "accessible-phylogenomic-mycobacterium-7489",
  "summary": "Agentic LLM systems are now tested on sequencing protocols, proteomics, transcriptomics, and gene-level evidence synthesis tasks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is embedding LLM agents in bounded, auditable analysis workflows across sequencing, proteomics and transcriptomics. LibStructBench evaluates whether agents reconstruct sequencing-library structures from documents. A conversational LC-MS/MS agent interrogates proteomics datasets through executable tests, controls and traceable records. A multi-model benchmark scores transcriptomic analysis of COVID-19 host responses for clustering, outliers and reproducibility. Other items extend this to infrastructure and design: GLESKit exposes gene-level evidence via MCP, LPT orchestrates bounded agentic loops over a curated literature database for binder design, and a harness-engineering study reports multi-agent hypothesis generation. A published commentary argues microbiome training needs AI competence beyond literacy. The novelty is evaluation against verifiable standards rather than demonstration alone, though the items do not establish comparative performance across these systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-30"
}
